I used the usual adjacent-swap idea here. Each pass moves the largest remaining value to the end. The `swapped` flag stops the loop when a full pass makes no changes.

For example, with `[5, 1, 4]`, the first pass gives `[1, 4, 5]`. The next pass makes no swap, so the method returns early.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        for (int end = n - 1; end > 0; --end) {
            bool swapped = false;

            for (int i = 0; i < end; ++i) {
                if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
                    swap(nums[i], nums[i + 1]);
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }

            if (!swapped) {
                break;
            }
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

The input vector is changed in place. Time complexity is `O(n^2)` in the worst case and `O(n)` when it is already sorted. Space complexity is `O(1)`.

Here the final swap position tells us how far the next pass needs to go. Everything after that position is already in order, so the boundary can jump back instead of moving only one place.

If no swap occurs, `lastSwap` stays `-1` and the array is finished. The method returns the sorted input array.

```javascript
class Solution {
    bubbleSort(nums) {
        let boundary = nums.length - 1;

        while (boundary > 0) {
            let lastSwap = -1;

            for (let i = 0; i < boundary; i++) {
                if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
                    const temp = nums[i];
                    nums[i] = nums[i + 1];
                    nums[i + 1] = temp;
                    lastSwap = i;
                }
            }

            if (lastSwap === -1) {
                break;
            }

            boundary = lastSwap;
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

An already sorted list and an empty list are handled without special calls. The array is changed in place. Time complexity is `O(n^2)` in the worst case, while already ordered data can finish in `O(n)`. Space complexity is `O(1)`.

Instead of writing both loops, this version uses recursion for the passes. One recursive call moves the largest value to the last unsorted position. Then the next call handles the shorter prefix.

The stopping check is important for empty and single-item vectors. The input vector is still sorted in place.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    void sortPrefix(vector<int>& nums, int length) {
        if (length <= 1) {
            return;
        }

        bool changed = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < length - 1; ++i) {
            if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
                swap(nums[i], nums[i + 1]);
                changed = true;
            }
        }

        if (changed) {
            sortPrefix(nums, length - 1);
        }
    }

public:
    vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        sortPrefix(nums, nums.size());
        return nums;
    }
};
```

For `[3, 1, 2]`, the first pass puts `3` at the end and leaves `[1, 2, 3]`. The next pass finds no swap, so recursion stops. Time complexity is `O(n^2)` in the worst case. The recursion uses `O(n)` stack space.

This version scans from the right side during every pass. When two neighboring values are out of order, they are exchanged. The smallest value found in that pass moves toward the front.

An empty array or an array with one value needs no work, and the loop handles both cases safely.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] bubbleSort(int[] nums) {
        for (int start = 0; start < nums.length - 1; start++) {
            boolean changed = false;

            for (int i = nums.length - 1; i > start; i--) {
                if (nums[i] < nums[i - 1]) {
                    int temp = nums[i];
                    nums[i] = nums[i - 1];
                    nums[i - 1] = temp;
                    changed = true;
                }
            }

            if (!changed) {
                break;
            }
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

For `[4, 2, 3]`, the first pass changes it to `[2, 3, 4]`. The next pass has no exchange, so sorting ends. The array is modified directly. Time complexity is `O(n^2)` worst case and `O(n)` for sorted input. Extra space is `O(1)`.

The inner loop does not need to check the part already placed at the end. After each pass, that sorted section grows by one item, so the boundary becomes smaller.

This is a simple in-place version. It does not create a second list, which also keeps the result easy to follow.

```python
class Solution:
    def bubbleSort(self, nums):
        last = len(nums) - 1

        while last > 0:
            for i in range(last):
                if nums[i] > nums[i + 1]:
                    nums[i], nums[i + 1] = nums[i + 1], nums[i]

            last -= 1

        return nums
```

The method also works for `[]` and `[7]`, because the loop is skipped. It changes the supplied list and returns that same list. Time complexity is `O(n^2)`. Space complexity is `O(1)`.

