## Intuition

The key insight is that in every pass, we compare **adjacent elements** and swap them if they are in the wrong order. Because of these swaps, the **largest unsorted element moves to the end** of the array after every pass.

## Approach

* Start with the first element and compare it with the next element.
* If `nums[j] > nums[j+1]`, swap them.
* Continue comparing adjacent elements until the end of the unsorted portion.
* After the first pass, the largest element reaches the last position.
* After the second pass, the second-largest element reaches its correct position.
* Therefore, after every pass, the unsorted portion becomes smaller.
* The outer loop runs `n - 1` times, while the inner loop runs only up to `n - i - 1`.
* The algorithm sorts the array **in-place**, so no extra array is required.


## Solution

```java
class Solution { 
    public int[] bubbleSort(int[] nums) { 

        int n = nums.length; 

        for(int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++){ 

            for(int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++){ 

                if(nums[j] > nums[j + 1]){ 
                    int temp = nums[j]; 
                    nums[j] = nums[j + 1]; 
                    nums[j + 1] = temp; 
                } 
            } 
        } 

        return nums; 
    } 
}
```

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:**
O(n²) — The outer loop runs `n - 1` times and the inner loop performs comparisons over the remaining unsorted portion. In the worst case, this results in approximately `n²` comparisons.

**Space Complexity:**
O(1) — Only one temporary variable is used for swapping. No extra array or data structure is created.
